❓Question
Given n non-negative integers representing an elevation map where the width of each bar is 1, calculate how much water can be trapped after raining.

Example
Input:
height = [0,1,0,2,1,0,1,3,2,1,2,1]
Output:
6
There are 6 units of rain water trapped between the bars.

Approach — Two Pointers
Use two pointers:
start → left side
end   → right side
Also maintain:
lMax → maximum height seen from the left
rMax → maximum height seen from the right
At every step:
Update lMax and rMax.
Compare lMax and rMax.
If lMax < rMax:
Water at start is lMax - height[start]
Move start++
Otherwise:
Water at end is rMax - height[end]
Move end--
The important idea is:
Water at a position depends on the smaller of the maximum height on its left and right.

✅Code
class Solution {
    public int trap(int[] height) {
        int lMax = 0;
        int rMax = 0;
        int ans = 0;
        int start = 0;
        int end = height.length - 1;
        while (start < end) {
            lMax = Math.max(lMax, height[start]);
            rMax = Math.max(rMax, height[end]);
            if (lMax < rMax) {
                ans += lMax - height[start];
                start++;
            } else {
                ans += rMax - height[end];
                end--;
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)
